comparing filing marks and register conventions.","category":"mental"},{"name":"Formal testimony","description":"Knows how to present a claim in the language and order the harbour court expects.","category":"social"}]},{"id":"chr_yusuf_albahr","name":"Yusuf al-Bahr","role":"supporting","home_location_id":"loc_envoy_house","description":"Yusuf al-Bahr is the eldest of the three foreign envoys, a seasoned negotiator whose fleet's merchants have waited through more than one uncertain season. He learned diplomacy from captains who measured promises against weather and provisions, and he carries himself with a patience that can feel like a rebuke. A silver-threaded beard frames his steady face; his indigo robe is plain beside the finery of the other delegations, but its cuffs are carefully mended. He believes a public agreement is worth more than a private advantage, though he has rarely had to choose between the two.","motivation":"Preserve his delegation's dignity and the trust of the captains who depend on his word.","goal":"Secure the first sailing slot through a ruling the other claimants will acknowledge, not merely endure.","flaw":"He uses patience to avoid making a clear concession, believing delay can keep every door open.","internal_conflict":"Yusuf wants to be regarded as an honorable negotiator, but fears that yielding will make his own people see restraint as weakness.","external_conflict":"His claim is challenged by two rival envoys, and the approaching monsoon leaves little time for a settlement that all three can accept.","arc":"Yusuf begins by insisting that patient negotiation can preserve both his claim and his reputation. When Mira challenges the filing order and asks each envoy to stand by the evidence in public, he chooses to accept a verifiable ruling even if it costs him the first slot. He ends with less control over the outcome but a stronger claim to the trust he wanted to protect.","personality_traits":["measured","diplomatic","proud","patient"],"abilities":[{"name":"Coalition bargaining","description":"Can find terms that let opposing captains accept a shared decision without pretending their interests are identical.","category":"social"}]},{"id":"chr_leon_varela","name":"Leon Varela","role":"antagonist","home_location_id":"loc_envoy_house","description":"Leon Varela is an envoy for a western fleet whose merchants have pledged costly pepper cargo against the next sailing season. He rose from a factor's household by learning that a claim is only as strong as the moment it reaches the ledger, and he treats courtesy as a tool for controlling a room. He is broad-shouldered, sharply groomed, and dressed in dark wool that clings in the port's wet heat. His confidence hides a genuine fear that a second lost season would ruin the people who sent him.","motivation":"Protect the investment and reputation of the merchants who entrusted him with their claim.","goal":"Have his delegation recognized as the earliest filer and secure the first sailing slot.","flaw":"He equates winning with competence and regards any concession as proof that he has failed those who rely on him.","internal_conflict":"Leon knows that his certainty exceeds what the disputed record can prove, but fears that acknowledging doubt will destroy his authority.","external_conflict":"His claim competes with two others filed in the same hour, while the unregistered-gift scandal threatens to make every court entry suspect.","arc":"Leon begins convinced that forceful certainty is the only way to defend his delegation. When Mira brings the conflicting marks before the claimants, he must choose between pressing a doubtful advantage and risking public exposure. He withdraws his unsupported assertion and ends more willing to distinguish his duty to his merchants from his need to appear infallible.","personality_traits":["commanding","impatient","resourceful","status-conscious"],"abilities":[{"name":"Negotiation","description":"Can frame a demand as a practical necessity and draw attention to what a rival risks by refusing it.","category":"social"}]},{"id":"chr_samira_bint_rashid","name":"Samira bint Rashid","role":"supporting","home_location_id":"loc_envoy_house","description":"Samira bint Rashid represents a trading delegation whose cargo and crew are ready to sail as soon as the pilots declare the monsoon turned. She has spent years managing accounts in a family of merchants, and knows how a delayed season can turn a sound bargain into a ruinous one. Quick-eyed and compact, she wears a rust-red veil pinned with a small brass clasp and keeps her hands clean of ink by carrying a wax tablet for notes. She is frank in private and exacting in public, but has learned to hide uncertainty behind sharp questions.","motivation":"Protect her delegation's cargo and the livelihoods tied to its return voyage.","goal":"Win a fair hearing for her filing and prevent the gift scandal from being used to dismiss her claim.","flaw":"She treats disclosure as surrender and withholds relevant information until she can control how it is received.","internal_conflict":"Samira wants an honest ruling, yet fears that revealing her delegation's informal dealings will give its rivals the advantage.","external_conflict":"Her claim is disputed by the other envoys, and the unregistered gifts in the pepper warehouses make any association with merchant favors dangerous.","arc":"Samira begins determined to reveal only what cannot be used against her. When Mira asks the envoys to account for their claims under the gift-register rule, Samira openly identifies a favor offered to her delegation and accepts scrutiny of it. She ends understanding that candor can cost leverage while still making a fair hearing possible.","personality_traits":["shrewd","direct","guarded","practical"],"abilities":[{"name":"Trade accounting","description":"Can trace how cargo delays, advances, and obligations affect the interests behind a sailing claim.","category":"mental"}]},{"id":"chr_karim_pilot","name":"Karim","role":"supporting","home_location_id":"loc_pilot_ghat","description":"Karim is a master pilot who has spent his working life reading the water and wind from the harbour steps. He was a young apprentice when the Ghat Oath became public rite after a pilot was bought to misread the sea, and he has never forgotten how quickly a private suspicion can endanger a whole fleet. Lean and weather-darkened, he wears a faded cotton wrap and keeps a small piece of polished wood for marking wind shifts. He speaks sparingly, preferring a witnessed declaration to a confident prediction.","motivation":"Keep the pilots' declaration worthy of the public trust bound to the Ghat Oath.","goal":"Declare the monsoon turned only when the sea gives clear signs, without letting the court's dispute make him delay or rush the rite.","flaw":"He hides behind procedure when a difficult judgment requires him to explain what he sees.","internal_conflict":"Karim believes the public rite protects the harbour, but worries that the court may use its form to disguise pressure on the pilots.","external_conflict":"The rival claims and the approaching wind turn make every hour consequential, while the Ghat Oath requires a public declaration that cannot be privately purchased.","arc":"Karim begins relying on the rite's formal steps to avoid being drawn into the court's conflict. When the wind shows a clear change as the envoys remain deadlocked, he declares what he observes before the assembled witnesses and explains the signs rather than letting silence serve as cover. He ends treating the Ghat Oath not only as protection from bribery but as a responsibility to speak plainly.","personality_traits":["watchful","disciplined","quiet","steadfast"],"abilities":[{"name":"Wind reading","description":"Reads local sea and wind signs to judge when the monsoon has turned, as required before any fleet may sail.","category":"mental"}]}],"relationships":[{"id":"rel_mira_yusuf","source_character_id":"chr_mira_das","target_character_id":"chr_yusuf_albahr","relationship_type":"cautious ally","description":"Mira Das respects Yusuf al-Bahr's willingness to negotiate, but suspects his patience can conceal a claim he will not readily yield."},{"id":"rel_leon_mira","source_character_id":"chr_leon_varela","target_character_id":"chr_mira_das","relationship_type":"rival","description":"Leon Varela needs Mira Das to certify the filing order, while she refuses to let his certainty substitute for evidence."},{"id":"rel_samira_leon","source_character_id":"chr_samira_bint_rashid","target_character_id":"chr_leon_varela","relationship_type":"rival","description":"Samira bint Rashid and Leon Varela compete for the first slot and distrust each other's accounts of the disputed filing hour."},{"id":"rel_yusuf_samira","source_character_id":"chr_yusuf_albahr","target_character_id":"chr_samira_bint_rashid","relationship_type":"rival","description":"Yusuf al-Bahr wants a settlement Samira bint Rashid can accept, but their delegations' claims cannot both hold the first place."},{"id":"rel_karim_mira","source_character_id":"chr_karim_pilot","target_character_id":"chr_mira_das","relationship_type":"ally","description":"Karim gives Mira Das a public account of the wind's signs, supporting her effort to keep the court's decision separate from pressure on the pilots."},{"id":"rel_samira_mira","source_character_id":"chr_samira_bint_rashid","target_character_id":"chr_mira_das","relationship_type":"uneasy ally","description":"Samira bint Rashid wants Mira Das to investigate the ledger, but worries that the scribe's inquiry will expose her delegation's own favors."}]}
