ical necessity and draw attention to what a rival risks by refusing it.","category":"social"}]},{"id":"chr_samira_bint_rashid","name":"Samira bint Rashid","role":"supporting","home_location_id":"loc_envoy_house","description":"Samira bint Rashid represents a trading delegation whose cargo and crew are ready to sail as soon as the pilots declare the monsoon turned. She has spent years managing accounts in a family of merchants, and knows how a delayed season can turn a sound bargain into a ruinous one. Quick-eyed and compact, she wears a rust-red veil pinned with a small brass clasp and keeps her hands clean of ink by carrying a wax tablet for notes. She is frank in private and exacting in public, but has learned to hide uncertainty behind sharp questions.","motivation":"Protect her delegation's cargo and the livelihoods tied to its return voyage.","goal":"Win a fair hearing for her filing and prevent the gift scandal from being used to dismiss her claim.","flaw":"She treats disclosure as surrender and withholds relevant information until she can control how it is received.","internal_conflict":"Samira wants an honest ruling, yet fears that revealing her delegation's informal dealings will give its rivals the advantage.","external_conflict":"Her claim is disputed by the other envoys, and the unregistered gifts in the pepper warehouses make any association with merchant favors dangerous.","arc":"Samira begins determined to reveal only what cannot be used against her. When Mira asks the envoys to account for their claims under the gift-register rule, Samira openly identifies a favor offered to her delegation and accepts scrutiny of it. She ends understanding that candor can cost leverage while still making a fair hearing possible.","personality_traits":["shrewd","direct","guarded","practical"],"abilities":[{"name":"Trade accounting","description":"Can trace how cargo delays, advances, and obligations affect the interests behind a sailing claim.","category":"mental"}]},{"id":"chr_karim_pilot","name":"Karim","role":"supporting","home_location_id":"loc_pilot_ghat","description":"Karim is a master pilot who has spent his working life reading the water and wind from the harbour steps. He was a young apprentice when the Ghat Oath became public rite after a pilot was bought to misread the sea, and he has never forgotten how quickly a private suspicion can endanger a whole fleet. Lean and weather-darkened, he wears a faded cotton wrap and keeps a small piece of polished wood for marking wind shifts. He speaks sparingly, preferring a witnessed declaration to a confident prediction.","motivation":"Keep the pilots' declaration worthy of the public trust bound to the Ghat Oath.","goal":"Declare the monsoon turned only when the sea gives clear signs, without letting the court's dispute make him delay or rush the rite.","flaw":"He hides behind procedure when a difficult judgment requires him to explain what he sees.","internal_conflict":"Karim believes the public rite protects the harbour, but worries that the court may use its form to disguise pressure on the pilots.","external_conflict":"The rival claims and the approaching wind turn make every hour consequential, while the Ghat Oath requires a public declaration that cannot be privately purchased.","arc":"Karim begins relying on the rite's formal steps to avoid being drawn into the court's conflict. When the wind shows a clear change as the envoys remain deadlocked, he declares what he observes before the assembled witnesses and explains the signs rather than letting silence serve as cover. He ends treating the Ghat Oath not only as protection from bribery but as a responsibility to speak plainly.","personality_traits":["watchful","disciplined","quiet","steadfast"],"abilities":[{"name":"Wind reading","description":"Reads local sea and wind signs to judge when the monsoon has turned, as required before any fleet may sail.","category":"mental"}]}],"relationships":[{"id":"rel_mira_yusuf","sour